---
// What a first class looks like, in three steps.
---

<h3 class="steps-title">What to expect</h3>
<p class="steps-lede">No uniform or equipment is required and no prior experience is necessary.</p>
<div class="steps">
  <div class="step">
    <p class="jp" lang="ja" aria-hidden="true">
      礼
    </p>
    <h3>Etiquette first</h3>
    <p class="muted">
      Practice begins with a bow to the shajo (shooting hall). You learn how to enter the hall and where to stand before
      you handle a bow.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <p class="jp" lang="ja" aria-hidden="true">
      型
    </p>
    <h3>Form before arrows</h3>
    <p class="muted">
      You learn the eight stages of shooting (shahō hassetsu) with a rubber practice bow called a gomuyumi before you
      draw a real one. Wear loose clothing you can move in.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <p class="jp" lang="ja" aria-hidden="true">
      的
    </p>
    <h3>Makiwara, then the mato</h3>
    <p class="muted">
      Next come the glove and your first arrows at the makiwara. That is a straw target shot at close range. Once your
      form is steady you move on to the mato at 28 metres.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
